---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { url } from '../lib/url'
import plugins from '../data/plugins.json'
import schema from '../data/schema.json'

const families = [
  { name: 'Commerce', blurb: 'Cart, checkout and order events as RUM custom actions, so revenue lands next to errors and slow views.', count: plugins.filter((p) => p.family === 'commerce').length },
  { name: 'Consent', blurb: 'Hold RUM until the visitor accepts analytics, read from the site’s own consent manager.', count: plugins.filter((p) => p.family === 'consent').length },
  { name: 'Identity', blurb: 'setUser and setAccount from the auth provider the app already runs.', count: plugins.filter((p) => p.family === 'identity').length },
]

const featured = plugins.slice(0, 3)
const actionCount = schema.families.reduce((total, family) => total + family.actions.length, 0)
---

<Base
  title="Business context for Datadog RUM, without custom code"
  description="Community-maintained Datadog Browser RUM plugins for commerce platforms, consent managers and login providers."
>
  <h1>Business context for Datadog RUM, without custom code</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The Browser RUM SDK collects sessions, views, errors and long tasks on its own. Who the shopper is,
    which account they belong to and what was in the cart do not come for free. These plugins read the
    platform’s own events and turn them into RUM context and custom actions, so order count and revenue
    become Datadog metrics without writing a line of tracking code.
  </p>

  <h2>Three steps</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Keep your existing RUM init.</strong> Plugins never call <code>init</code> themselves, so your
      application ID, sampling and privacy settings stay in charge.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Add a plugin to <code>plugins: [...]</code>.</strong> Consent adapters go first, so nothing is
      collected before the visitor accepts.
      <pre><code>{`import { datadogRum } from '@datadog/browser-rum'
import { oneTrustPlugin } from '@kyletaylored/datadog-rum-plugin-onetrust'
import { wooCommercePlugin } from '@kyletaylored/datadog-rum-plugin-woocommerce'

datadogRum.init({
  applicationId, clientToken, site, service, env, version,
  plugins: [
    oneTrustPlugin({ category: 'C0002' }),
    wooCommercePlugin({ identifyUsers: true }),
  ],
})`}</code></pre>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Import the asset pack.</strong> {actionCount} actions across {schema.families.length} families
      feed one <a href={url('/docs/datadog-assets/')}>dashboard and monitor set</a> that works on every platform.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    The adapter is the only platform-specific part. Everything below it is shared: order dedupe, the consent
    gate, the PII and payment-data filter, size caps and the debug log all live in the core, so a new adapter
    inherits them without reimplementing any of it.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <pre><code>{`Platform event source        Shopify Web Pixels · WooCommerce DOM events · OneTrust · Auth0 · …
        ↓
Platform adapter             one package per platform; maps events onto the shared schema
        ↓
Shared core                  dedupe · consent gate · PII and payment filter · size caps · debug log
        ↓
RUM SDK public API           setUser / setAccount · setGlobalContextProperty · addAction
        ↓
Datadog                      RUM Explorer, Session Replay, generated metrics, dashboard, monitors`}</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h2>Plugin families</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {families.map((family) => (
      <div class="card">
        <h3>{family.name}</h3>
        <p>{family.blurb}</p>
        <div class="badges">
          <span class="badge">{family.count} available</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>Featured plugins</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {featured.map((plugin) => (
      <div class="card">
        <h3><a href={url(`/plugins/${plugin.name}/`)}>{plugin.title ?? plugin.name}</a></h3>
        <p>{plugin.description}</p>
        <div class="badges">
          <span class={`badge ${plugin.tier}`}>{plugin.tier === 'seed' ? 'Maintainer' : plugin.tier}</span>
          <span class="badge">{plugin.family}</span>
          <span class="badge">v{plugin.version}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p><a href={url('/plugins/')}>Browse all plugins →</a></p>

  <h2>What it will not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No payment data, ever.</strong> Adapters read platform event payloads, never DOM inputs, and card fields are stripped in the core even if a payload carries them.</li>
    <li><strong>No PII by default.</strong> <code>usr.id</code> is the platform customer ID; email and name need an explicit opt-in. Search terms are sent as a length, never the text.</li>
    <li><strong>No claim to replace order reporting.</strong> These numbers come from the browser and undercount. They show trends and anomalies next to performance and errors; the platform’s admin report stays the source of truth.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
